(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-ans-cholinergic-quiz"]={"title":"ANS and Cholinergic Drugs Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What do the bladder antimuscarinics do, per the lecture?","choices":["They lower bladder pressure and raise bladder capacity","Raise voiding pressure and decrease bladder capacity","Relax the trigone and sphincter to promote voiding","Have no bladder effect and are used for drooling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is the mirror image of bethanechol's action. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which agents does the lecture name for Alzheimer disease, and what is the rationale?","choices":["Pralidoxime and dantrolene, because they restore enzyme function","Donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine","Physostigmine, neostigmine and pyridostigmine, because they cross into the brain","Atropine, scopolamine and glycopyrrolate, because they reduce secretions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and their common adverse effect is gastrointestinal distress. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter does the lecture describe as the major inhibitory transmitter in the brain?","choices":["Norepinephrine, which regulates normal brain processes","Gamma-aminobutyric acid","Glycine, the inhibitory transmitter of spinal cord neurons","Acetylcholine, which controls memory in brain neurons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, while glycine is given as the inhibitory transmitter used by spinal cord neurons. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"From which plants does the lecture say atropine is derived?","choices":["Foxglove and lily of the valley","Opium poppy and coca","Ephedra and willow bark","Deadly nightshade, jimson weed and mandrake"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and scopolamine comes from the same family. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say competitive neuromuscular blockers significantly increased the safety of anaesthesia?","choices":["They shorten the duration of surgery","They eliminate the need for intubation","They prevent malignant hyperthermia","They allow the use of less anaesthetic agent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The relaxation is achieved without deepening the anaesthetic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which additional effect does carbachol produce through its nicotinic action?","choices":["Release of epinephrine from the adrenal medulla","Direct blockade of the neuromuscular junction","Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase","Depletion of stored norepinephrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why its cardiovascular and gastrointestinal effects can be profound and biphasic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is pyridostigmine's clinical role in the lecture?","choices":["Chronic management of myasthenia gravis","Diagnosis of myasthenia gravis","Reactivating acetylcholinesterase after organophosphate exposure","Reducing intraocular pressure in glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, as distinct from edrophonium's diagnostic role. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What does the lecture say norepinephrine acts as?","choices":["Both a neurotransmitter and a hormone","A neurotransmitter only, with no hormonal role","A hormone only, released from the adrenal cortex","An inhibitory transmitter in pain pathways of the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it also regulates normal brain processes, usually excitatory. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which terms does the lecture give as synonymous with cholinergic antagonists?","choices":["Anticholinergics, parasympatholytics or antimuscarinics","Cholinomimetics and parasympathomimetics","Sympatholytics and adrenergic blockers","Anticholinesterases and indirect-acting agents"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, while the agonists are cholinomimetics or parasympathomimetics. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is carbachol's clinical use in the lecture, and what receptor activity does it have?","choices":["Lowering intraocular pressure in glaucoma","Stimulating an atonic bladder, with muscarinic activity only","Treating xerostomia, with muscarinic activity only","Diagnosing myasthenia gravis, by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it causes miosis and spasm of accommodation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about neostigmine's absorption and central penetration?","choices":["It penetrates the central nervous system and is used for Alzheimer disease","Poorly absorbed orally, and it does not enter the central nervous system","It is well absorbed orally and readily enters the central nervous system","It is given only intravenously because it is destroyed by gastric acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is what keeps its effects peripheral. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How does the lecture divide the efferent division of the peripheral nervous system?","choices":["Into the brain and the spinal cord, which together form the outflow","Somatic, under voluntary control, and autonomic, under involuntary control","Into the afferent system, carrying signals to the centre, and the enteric system, acting independently","Into the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems only, with no somatic component"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Afferent carries signals to the central nervous system; efferent carries them away and splits this way. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does the lecture say happens with nicotine at autonomic ganglia?","choices":["It has no ganglionic action and acts only in the central nervous system","It permanently destroys ganglionic receptors","It depolarizes the ganglia, first stimulating and then paralysing them","It competitively and reversibly blocks them from the outset"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the stimulation raises blood pressure, heart rate, peristalsis and secretions before the fall. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A genetically susceptible patient given succinylcholine with halothane develops muscular rigidity, metabolic acidosis, tachycardia and a very high temperature. What does the lecture direct?","choices":["Atropine to oppose muscarinic overactivity","Neostigmine to reverse the blockade","Rapid cooling and dantrolene","Pralidoxime to reactivate acetylcholinesterase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and blocking that release is what reduces heat production. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A patient is agitated and hallucinating, with dilated pupils, flushed dry skin, fever, urinary retention and a fast heart. Which toxidrome is this?","choices":["Muscarinic, from a cholinergic agonist","Nicotinic, from ganglionic stimulation","Beta blocker overdose","Anticholinergic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives it as mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet, dry as a bone, hot as Hades, with retention and tachycardia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which antisecretory use does the lecture give for atropine?","choices":["Reducing gastric acid secretion in peptic ulcer disease","Reducing bronchial secretions in asthma maintenance","Preventing sweating in axillary hyperhidrosis","Blocking respiratory tract secretions before surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and glycopyrrolate is used similarly before surgery. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the enteric nervous system?","choices":["It innervates the gut, pancreas and gallbladder and functions independently","It innervates only the stomach and is entirely dependent on vagal input","It is a purely sensory system carrying pain from the abdomen","It is a subdivision of the somatic system controlling abdominal wall muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is called the brain of the gut. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which set matches atropine's adverse effects in the lecture?","choices":["Fasciculations, weakness and hypertension","Sedation, constipation and rebound hypertension","Dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention and constipation","Salivation, lacrimation, diarrhoea and bronchospasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with bradycardia at lower doses and tachycardia at higher ones. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise dopamine?","choices":["The major inhibitory transmitter of the brain","Involved chiefly in mood, appetite and sensory perception","Multiple functions by location, and usually inhibitory","Purely excitatory, controlling muscle movement in spinal cord neurons"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which contrasts with acetylcholine being usually excitatory. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which sequence describes neurotransmission at a cholinergic neuron in the lecture?","choices":["Synthesis, storage, release, and inactivation by catechol-O-methyltransferase","Storage, release, binding, and permanent removal of choline from the neuron","Synthesis, storage, release, receptor binding, degradation, recycling of choline","Synthesis, release, reuptake into the presynaptic terminal, and repackaging without degradation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Six sequential steps, and several drug classes act at one of them. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which set of cardiovascular effects does the lecture give for acetylcholine itself?","choices":["An increase in heart rate with a rise in blood pressure","No cardiovascular effect, since its action is confined to glands","A rise in cardiac output with a fall in heart rate","A decrease in heart rate, cardiac output and blood pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside increased salivary, intestinal and bronchiolar secretions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does the lecture call the two phases of succinylcholine's action?","choices":["Phase one, competitive blockade, and phase two, irreversible binding","Phase one, enzyme inhibition, and phase two, enzyme reactivation","Phase one, histamine release, and phase two, bronchoconstriction","Phase one depolarization, then phase two resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the receptor becomes incapable of transmitting further impulses. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which three systems make up the autonomic division in the lecture?","choices":["Central, peripheral and enteric","Enteric, parasympathetic and sympathetic","Somatic, parasympathetic and sympathetic","Afferent, efferent and enteric"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The enteric system is the one most often forgotten of the three. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What happens to acetylcholine after it binds its receptor?","choices":["It is metabolised by monoamine oxidase within the neuron","It diffuses away without enzymatic breakdown","It is degraded by acetylcholinesterase, and the choline is recycled","It is taken back into the presynaptic terminal intact and repackaged"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Degradation in the cleft, not reuptake of the intact transmitter, is the cholinergic pattern. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which effects does the lecture give as predominating with ganglionic blockade?","choices":["Miosis with salivation and diarrhoea","Vasodilation, with atony of the bladder and gut and tachycardia","Vasoconstriction with bradycardia and increased secretions","Bronchodilation with hyperglycaemia and tremor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, since each organ loses whichever division normally dominates it. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Why does the lecture suggest a small dose of a nondepolarizing blocker before succinylcholine?","choices":["To allow a lower dose of anaesthetic agent","To prevent the fasciculations that cause muscle soreness","To prolong the duration of the block","To prevent malignant hyperthermia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a comfort measure that addresses a predictable adverse effect. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What are atropine's effects on the eye?","choices":["No ocular effect at any dose","Persistent mydriasis and cycloplegia","Miosis with spasm of accommodation","Reduced aqueous humour production with no change in pupil size"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is the opposite of the miosis and accommodation spasm the agonists cause. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which three classes make up the cholinergic antagonists in the lecture?","choices":["Alpha blockers, beta blockers and mixed blockers","Choline esters, alkaloids and anticholinesterases","Antimuscarinic agents, ganglionic blockers and neuromuscular blocking agents","Direct-acting agents, indirect-acting agents and reactivators"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they are distinguished by which cholinergic site they occupy. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which three general types of signalling between cells does the lecture list?","choices":["Hormones, enzymes and ion channels","Neurotransmitters, receptors and second messengers","Afferent, efferent and enteric signalling","Hormones, local mediators and neurotransmitters"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the neurotransmitters of interest here are acetylcholine, norepinephrine and epinephrine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which uses does the lecture list for neostigmine?","choices":["Stimulating the bladder and gut, and reversing competitive blockade","Diagnosing myasthenia gravis and assessing cholinesterase therapy","Slowing the progression of Alzheimer disease","Treating an overdose of an anticholinergic drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it can stimulate contractility before paralysing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"}]};
